Source: Octopus Energy, “Agile pricing explained” (https://octopus.energy/blog/agile-pricing-explained).

Its illustrative example states: “The Agile price is 2.2 times the wholesale price of energy” with “an additional 12p / kWh” between 4.00pm and 7.00pm, “capped at 100p / kWh”.

Its regional table publishes these D and P values, quoted without conversion:
East England: D “2.10”, P “13”;
East Midlands: D “2.00”, P “14”;
London: D “2.00”, P “12”;
Merseyside & Northern Wales: D “2.20”, P “13”;
West Midlands: D “2.10”, P “12”;
North East England: D “2.10”, P “12”;
North West England: D “2.10”, P “12”;
South England: D “2.10”, P “12”;
South East England: D “2.20”, P “12”;
South Wales: D “2.20”, P “12”;
South West England: D “2.30”, P “11”;
Yorkshire: D “2.00”, P “13”;
South Scotland: D “2.10”, P “13”;
North Scotland: D “2.40”, P “12”.

The displayed formula is “min(D x W + P, 95)”. The source explicitly explains: “95 is chosen to ensure the price is capped at 100p/kWh once VAT is added.”
